Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
five hundred  sixty seven thousand seven hundred and  fifteen  that's how many homeless people the u.s  department of housing and urban  development  counted on a cold january night in 2019.  more than half a million americans are  homeless on any given night  but on the west coast the problem has  grown worse in recent years  things haven't improved with the coven  19 pandemic in 2020  homelessness is on the rise in america  san francisco is rushing to contain a  coronavirus outbreak that hit its  biggest homeless shelter the homeless  population in the county is up 13  percent from last year  as the city of l.a saw a 14 jump  homelessness is growing  and governments have done  too little too late  there's no doubt that the homeless are  stigmatized and all too often we  conflate the issue with mental health  or we just blame the victim most people  blame homelessness on the person  that it's they're a bum or they're lazy  or they made bad decisions if  homelessness was a punishment for bad  decisions we'd all be homeless but this  is a complex  issue and no two stories are alike there  are actually  a huge range of forces that  often kind of come together and compound  one another when it comes to how an  individual  falls into homelessness but how did we  get here how did a nation  with such wealth and prosperity wind up  with half a million people on the  streets  and more importantly what can we do  about it it really is an urgent problem  and there's no reason  that we shouldn't fix it i zoomed in  with a journalist  two non-profit execs and a formally  displaced activist  to better understand the confounding  issue of america's homeless  before we can understand american  homelessness we need to know a little  bit about its origins  in america a lot of scholars look to the  period immediately following the civil  war  as the emergence of homelessness a lot  of veterans were returning from the war  and returning to very overcrowded you  know industrializing cities  that were very short on housing ever  since then there's been a population of  people  in america who have not necessarily fit  into the sort of  standard economic narrative fields like  logging  fishing mining they wouldn't really have  what we would think of as a stable home  many people who were  what we would call homeless actually did  have shelter  in places like single room occupancy  apartments and hotels  um in what they would call flop houses  in the 1950s and 60s there was  at that time urban renewal in cities so  they were just numerically speaking a  lot fewer of these sort of  transitional very inexpensive housing  options  a lot of low-income minority  neighborhoods in american cities  were ripped apart a lot of the housing  that existed in those neighborhoods  was also gone you have more  de-institutionalization  of people experiencing mental illness  starting in the late 70s and 80s the  department of housing and urban  development  is getting defunded to a significant  degree and then just  de-industrialization  and rising economic inequality created a  more  a larger population of people that are  sort of living in extreme poverty  now that of course is a very abridged  version of the history of american  homelessness  but the main takeaway seems to be that  there is no one cause of the problem  the reason that people fall into  homelessness is that  there's basically a series of  unfortunate events that  befall them some of those include losing  a job  they include domestic violence that's a  leading  issue for women in particular and  families returning from war  and experiencing post-traumatic stress  losing  benefits whether health care or welfare  having a health problem that costs a  huge amount of money  and bankrupts somebody if you mix one of  those things  or you know add multiple of those forces  together  add in addiction or mental health issues  while a multitude of factors can and do  contribute to homelessness  what it really boils down to is right in  the name  homes the truth is the leading cause of  homelessness is the lack of affordable  housing  people are not earning enough money to  pay for the housing that's available  essentially  there are approximately 11 million  low-income renters in america  research from the federal reserve found  that 59  of americans are one paycheck away from  being homeless  for every 10 of the lowest income  renters  there's fewer than four apartments that  are affordable to them  and so because there's such a shortage  we have  nearly eight million of the lowest  income renter  households families who are paying at  least half of their income  towards their rent and many are paying  much more they're paying  60 70 80 of their income just to keep a  roof  over their heads so it's sort of like a  game of musical chairs  um you have the chairs the chairs are  getting pulled away  and they're just more people than there  are chairs to sit in coven 19 has proven  to be  the straw that broke the camel's back  for many of these struggling households  i would say that coveted 19 has  exacerbated  the underlying crisis of affordable  housing  and the tremendous racial disparities in  who is most  harmed by the housing crisis when you  have such limited income to begin with  and you're paying so much of it to keep  a roof over your head  you're always one financial shock  away from missing rent and potentially  facing eviction and in worse cases  becoming homeless  so for many of these same households  the coronavirus is that financial  emergency  they've lost jobs they've lost hours at  work they've lost wages  it's harder than ever for them to cobble  together what they need to make rent  a columbia university economist  predicted a 40 to 45 percent increase in  homelessness by the end of 2020  this is due to both the newly displaced  as well as the strain coven 19 has put  on existing homeless communities covet  has had  definitely had an effect on the  homelessness system shelters  have had to follow the cdc guidance on  social distancing so  they've essentially had to decompress or  thin out  their shelters they can't serve as many  people often they're serving half the  number of people that they used to serve  but there is a silver lining here cloven  19 while devastating  has demonstrated what's possible when we  mobilize to help the most vulnerable  among us  there's also now a lot more creative  thinking  around what to do to create more housing  and more opportunities for  safe places for homeless people to to  stay there's been  more of an embrace of sanctioned  encampments  which are essentially places where  local governments set up security and  bathrooms and facilities to help  and then allow homeless people to come  the other big thing is hotels were  emptied out  because tourists disappeared during  covid and so  a lot of cities started housing homeless  people in in those empty hotels  hundreds of men have moved from homeless  shelters to a fancy hotel  i called the lucerne hotel they said  they are not taking reservations  until at least december it seems like  that was  a pretty successful way to actually  prevent mass outbreaks among the  homeless population  one thing the coronavirus has shown is  that governments  if they want to can work with homeless  service providers  and increase the urgency  and create programs to get people off  the streets  but clearly we as a country  are not doing enough we can end  homelessness  we actually know how to end homelessness  and we've known how to end homelessness  for a long time the problem is there's  not the public  and political will to push  all the policy change forward to  create more affordable housing and to  create  programs in support of housing to get  homeless people  off the streets in the numbers that we  need  to get them off the streets to end  homelessness  i think there is a widespread sense  among people who work  in homelessness services that there  isn't enough  money devoted to this and  the the top line answer is that  it's such a big and complicated problem  that there are several  levers that need to be pulled from a  policy perspective  there's generally bipartisan  understanding  that we have a homelessness crisis that  is unacceptable  there's generally a bipartisan  understanding that we have a shortage  of affordable apartments for the lowest  income people  where there tends to be a split is  whether  there's a willingness to fund the  investments that are needed to  alleviate the problems the major way  that you're going to end homelessness by  getting people into housing as quickly  as possible  and since these are very poor people and  they can't afford  most housing we're going to need housing  subsidies  to accomplish that many many people the  majority of people who become homeless  actually do exit homelessness they get  back on their feet  they find a job and they don't enter the  homeless system again  and it's not a long-term lifetime  sentence  being homeless so we have to remember  that it's something that for most people  is a shorter term experience  you

